"Like I heard a feller sayin': the Eye-talians built New York, the Irish run it an' the Jews own it."
That's a typically casual (if comparatively mild) racial smear offered up by the assorted neighbors gathered out on the front stoop in "Street Scene," Elmer Rice's seldom revived 1929 Pulitzer winner about New York's tenement neighborhoods and the various immigrant ethnicities teeming therein.
At the height of his career, Rice's popularity rivaled that of Eugene O'Neill, and though he had something of a reputation as a hothead, he was nothing if not an experimenter of form, including with "The Adding Machine," his expressionist play cannily adapted into a musical by Next Theatre in 2007.
