One of Scotland Yard's most senior officers yesterday apologised for the "inconvenience and disturbance" caused by a large-scale raid after intelligence that a chemical bomb attack was being planned in Britain.
But Andy Hayman, assistant commissioner of special operations at the Metropolitan police, warned that while the current terrorist threat existed there would be similar raids in London.
Mr Hayman appealed for calm during a demonstration in east London today in protest against the anti-terrorist operation a week ago on a family home in Forest Gate.
"We would encourage people to demonstrate as they should freely do so in this country," he said. But he added: "We must pull together in a caring and positive manner. This is not the time for conflict or anger."
The two men arrested in last Friday's 4am raid, Mohammed Abul Kaher, 23, and his brother, Abul Koyair, 20, remained in custody at Paddington Green police station yesterday where they continued to be questioned. Mr Kaher suffered a gunshot wound to his shoulder during the operation, which is the subject of an Independent Police Complaints Commission investigation.
No 46 Lansdown Road remained cordoned off yesterday as officers searched for what intelligence suggested was a chemical device in the house. But so far nothing has been found and the search is likely to conclude within two days.
Mr Hayman yesterday told the Muslim community in Forest Gate: "I am aware we have caused disruption and inconvenience to many residents and for that I apologise." But he went on: "Similar police operations will be required while the threat of terrorism continues."
He said senior officers were reviewing the way anti-terrorism raids were carried out. "The concern that has been expressed to me has been about the impact, the visual impact of the operation. That has caused some reflection as to whether or not we can do it differently. But having said that, there is a difficult balancing act... that makes it very difficult to see how we can reduce the level of officer attendance and equipment. We are planning some meetings with the community to share with them our planning and options."
But Mr Hayman refused to apologise for the raid after intelligence received left counter-terrorism officers with "no option" but to intervene.
The two men arrested can be interviewed until 4pm tomorrow but anti-terrorist officers may apply for an extension of their detention to the 14-day limit.
A demonstration planned today in Forest Gate could attract hundreds of people angry at the scale of the police operation and the lack of evidence that the intelligence they were working on was correct.
